  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {4}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "If we let $u=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,\u27f9\\,-\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {4}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}2\\cdot {4}^{u}(-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{0}{4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{{4}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}){|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}({4}^{0}-{4}^{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}(1-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
